# Backtracking
Search by trying, and undoing
14 Nov 2026

Shaun Chong

## Problems made of choices

Some problems ask for a set of choices that together obey some rules.

- Place eight queens so that none attacks another
- Fill in a Sudoku grid
- Find a path through a maze
- Pick items that add up to an exact total
- Timetable exams so no student has two at once

The number of combinations explodes. Listing every one and checking it is
hopeless beyond toy sizes.

: These are constraint satisfaction problems. Many are NP-complete, so there is no known fast general method; backtracking is the standard exact approach.

## The idea

Build the answer one choice at a time, and check the rules after every choice.

- If the partial answer can still work, make the next choice
- If it already breaks a rule, undo the last choice and try the next option
- If a choice has no options left, undo the choice before it

That undo is the *back* in backtracking. A dead end found early rules out every
answer that would have started the same way.

: The term was coined by the mathematician D. H. Lehmer in the 1950s.
: Compare generate-and-test: build every complete answer, then check it. Backtracking checks partial answers, so one failed check discards a whole family of complete answers at once.

## The search tree

Every partial answer is a node and every choice is an edge. Here are the
permutations of `abc`:

.image tree.svg _ 980

Backtracking walks this tree depth first, in the order of the grey numbers. It
only holds the current path in memory, never the whole tree.

: The green leaves are complete answers. In a real problem, most branches are cut off long before they reach a leaf.

## The pattern

Every backtracking search has the same shape: choose, explore, unchoose.

    func search(partial) {
        if complete(partial) {
            record(partial)
            return
        }
        for _, c := range candidates(partial) {
            if !ok(partial, c) {
                continue // prune: no answer starts this way
            }
            choose(partial, c)
            search(partial)
            unchoose(partial, c)
        }
    }

: Only three pieces change between problems: what the candidates are, how to check a partial answer, and how to undo a choice.
: Recursion keeps the current path on the call stack, so unchoose runs on the way back out.

## Example: permutations

No rules to prune on yet. It prints each node as it visits it. Press Run.

.play permutations.go /^func permute/,/^}/

: The output matches the numbered tree two slides back.
: path and used are shared by every call. That is why unchoose must restore them: the next sibling has to see the state from before this choice.

## Example: eight queens

Place eight queens on a chessboard so that no two share a row, column or
diagonal. The chess composer Max Bezzel posed it in 1848.

As a backtracking search:

- Place one queen per row, top to bottom
- In each row, try the columns from left to right
- Skip any square that a queen above already attacks
- If a row has no safe square, go back up and move the previous queen along

: Gauss took an interest in the puzzle. Franz Nauck published solutions in 1850 and generalised it to n queens on an n × n board.

## Four queens by hand

The same search on a 4 × 4 board. Crosses mark squares already under attack.

.image queens4.svg _ 980

: Two dead ends and two backtracks, then a solution.
: Between frames 1 and 2, row 1 also tried columns 0 and 1. Both were attacked, so the check rejected them without going any deeper.

## Eight queens in Go

`safe` checks the column and both diagonals against the queens above. Press Run.

.play queens.go /^func place/,/^}/

: It finds all 92 solutions and prints the first one.
: There is no explicit undo: the next column simply overwrites cols[row], and no row below is read until it is filled in again.

## Pruning pays

Boards each approach looks at to find all 92 solutions:

- Any 8 of the 64 squares, then check: 4,426,165,368
- One queen per row, then check: 16,777,216
- One per row and column, then check: 40,320
- Backtracking, checking each queen as it is placed: 2,057

The earlier a check rejects a partial answer, the bigger the subtree the search
never has to visit.

: The 2,057 counts partial boards too: 8 with one queen, 42 with two, 140 with three, then 344, 568, 550, 312, and the 92 complete ones.

## Example: Sudoku

Each empty cell is a choice of nine digits; the rows, columns and boxes are the rules.

.code sudoku.go /^func solve/,/^}/

: allowed checks the cell's row, column and 3 × 3 box for the digit.
: Returning true at the first solution stops the whole search. To count solutions instead, keep going and return false.

## Choosing what to try next

Two ways to pick the next empty cell, on Arto Inkala's 2012 puzzle, reported at
the time as the world's hardest Sudoku. Press Run.

- **First empty**: the next blank cell in reading order
- **Most constrained**: the blank cell with the fewest digits still allowed

.play sudoku.go /^func main/,/^}/

: Most constrained first finds dead ends sooner: a cell with no options fails at once, and a cell with one option is a forced move that costs no branching.
: Each call is more expensive because it scans the whole grid, but there are about five times fewer calls.

## Where you meet backtracking

- **Puzzles**: Sudoku, crosswords, pentominoes, chess problems
- **Constraint solvers**: timetables, staff rosters, product configuration
- **SAT solvers**: DPLL (1962) is backtracking with extra deduction at each step
- **Prolog**: running a program is a backtracking search through its rules
- **Regex engines**: Perl, Python, Java, JavaScript and PCRE all backtrack
- **Parsers** that try one grammar rule and fall back to the next

: Modern SAT solvers (CDCL) add clause learning and can jump back several levels at once instead of one.
: Knuth's Dancing Links is backtracking with a data structure that makes each undo cheap. It is a standard way to solve exact-cover puzzles such as Sudoku and pentominoes.

## When it bites

Pruning helps, but the worst case is still exponential. Take the regex
`^(a+)+b` on a run of a's with no b: each extra a doubles the ways to split the
a's into groups, and a backtracking engine tries every way before giving up.
Press Run.

.play regex.go /^func group/,/^}/

: In July 2019 a regex containing .*(?:.*=.*) backtracked so heavily that CPUs across Cloudflare's network sat near 100% for 27 minutes.
: Go's regexp package does not backtrack. It guarantees time linear in the input and gives up backreferences to do so; the last line of the output shows it.

## Takeaways

- Backtracking is a depth-first search over partial answers that undoes each
  choice on the way back out
- You supply three things: the candidates, a check, and an undo
- How early the check rejects, and which choice you try first, decide the speed
- The worst case is still exponential, so know which inputs could make it explode

: If the same subproblem keeps coming back, cache its answer: that is dynamic programming. Backtracking fits when the rules make most branches fail early.
